I rewrote `src/fom_solver.c` and `make verify` prints `VERIFY OK`. The worst error over 8 samples is 1.9e-12, against a tolerance of 1e-6. I didn't run `make bench`, so I have no timing numbers.

How the solver works:
- **Parameter terms computed once per solve:** the four `p0`/`p1` products are worked out before the loop. Each iteration only builds the two biases as `b + tau·q` and `b + eta·q`, which is 64 multiply-adds.
- **One provided routine per step:** `t0` and `t1` each go through their fused `fom_step_*` routine, which applies the relu or clip. The state update calls `fom_fused_su__rest` on the 144 computed rows. The 16 copy rows (128..143) are written directly from `state[0..15]`.
- **No copy each iteration:** `state` and `new_state` swap roles every iteration instead of being copied. At the end the result is copied back into `state` if needed.
- **Residual on the last iteration only:** the max-abs residual is computed only then, because this method has no tolerance check (`FOM_USE_TOL` is 0).
- **Constraints met:** no `/`, no dynamic allocation, no variable-length arrays, and no matrix data read directly. It uses only the workspace buffers. To make room, `solution.x` holds the precomputed parameter terms during the solve, and the final copy overwrites them. Each solve restarts from `FOM_STATE_INIT`, and so do `fom_setup` and `fom_update_params`.

A few claude.ai connectors (Asana, Atlassian, Box, Canva, Figma, HubSpot, Intercom, Linear, Notion, monday.com) need authorizing in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. This task didn't need any of them.
